1
00:00:14,894 --> 00:00:18,647
[HUDSON]
Just one moment, Sherlock!

2
00:00:18,689 --> 00:00:21,274
[HOLMES] Miss Hudson,
I didn't see you looming there.

3
00:00:21,316 --> 00:00:24,277
Even a day as beautiful as this
is nothing compared to you.

4
00:00:24,319 --> 00:00:27,322
Look at the time! Bye!

5
00:00:27,364 --> 00:00:29,950
Just one moment!

6
00:00:29,992 --> 00:00:31,493
Give it!

7
00:00:31,535 --> 00:00:32,912
Fine!

8
00:00:35,039 --> 00:00:37,583
Don't you start with me,
you no good apple thief!

9
00:00:37,624 --> 00:00:39,501
[HOLMES] Please!
I just need a little longer!

10
00:00:39,543 --> 00:00:40,794
Any day now, I'm sure of it!

11
00:00:40,836 --> 00:00:43,422
Too bad you can't pay
rent with excuses!

12
00:00:43,463 --> 00:00:45,799
If you don't have
any money, get a real job!

13
00:00:45,841 --> 00:00:48,761
Otherwise, I'm kicking you out
of your room, you understand?

14
00:00:48,802 --> 00:00:50,387
I do have a job, Miss Hudson!

15
00:00:50,429 --> 00:00:52,973
But you know it operates
on its own schedule!

16
00:00:53,015 --> 00:00:54,850
So, waiting sort of is my job.

17
00:00:54,892 --> 00:00:56,602
- -[HUDSON yells]
- -[STAMFORD] Holmes!

18
00:00:56,643 --> 00:00:58,771
[STAMFORD]
I thought that was you!

19
00:00:58,812 --> 00:01:02,649
What a surprise seeing you
grovelling on the sidewalk.

20
00:01:02,691 --> 00:01:04,276
Impeccable timing!

21
00:01:04,317 --> 00:01:06,486
Miss Hudson!
This is my good friend.

22
00:01:06,528 --> 00:01:08,530
You can call me Stamford, ma'am.

23
00:01:08,572 --> 00:01:10,616
I work at Saint
Bartholomew's Hospital.

24
00:01:10,657 --> 00:01:12,076
I'm a doctor, you see.

25
00:01:12,117 --> 00:01:14,536
Well, it's a pleasure
to meet you!

26
00:01:14,578 --> 00:01:15,955
[STAMFORD grunts]

27
00:01:15,996 --> 00:01:19,708
Now that that's settled,
lend me some money.

28
00:01:22,044 --> 00:01:24,713
I see. Quite the situation.

29
00:01:24,755 --> 00:01:27,340
What did I do to
deserve such abuse?

30
00:01:27,382 --> 00:01:29,927
Don't be cross.
It'll give you more wrinkles.

31
00:01:29,969 --> 00:01:31,261
Give me more?!

32
00:01:31,303 --> 00:01:34,056
I'd have nary a one if I
didn't have to deal up with you!

33
00:01:34,098 --> 00:01:37,935
Now children, if I may,
it seems the logical solution

34
00:01:37,977 --> 00:01:39,394
would be to find
Holmes a flatmate

35
00:01:39,436 --> 00:01:41,187
with whom he
could split the rent.

36
00:01:41,229 --> 00:01:44,650
[BOTH]
Hm? A flatmate?

37
00:03:19,452 --> 00:03:22,164
If you're ready, I've found
some candidates for you

38
00:03:22,206 --> 00:03:24,332
to consider as possible tenants.

39
00:03:24,374 --> 00:03:26,626
Ahh. Like an audition.

40
00:03:26,668 --> 00:03:28,294
We'll see what they can offer.

41
00:03:28,336 --> 00:03:30,589
Then I'll bring out
our first victim.

42
00:03:32,549 --> 00:03:35,301
Oh, and what do
I do for a living?

43
00:03:35,343 --> 00:03:37,763
Just working on becoming
the next big actor.

44
00:03:37,804 --> 00:03:40,015
I know! You could be my patron.

45
00:03:40,057 --> 00:03:42,809
I'll pay back any rent
as soon as I make it.

46
00:03:42,851 --> 00:03:45,311
Tempting, but I shall pass.

47
00:03:45,353 --> 00:03:46,312
I heard you wouldn't mind

48
00:03:46,354 --> 00:03:48,107
if I brought strange
men back to my room

49
00:03:48,148 --> 00:03:50,442
as long as it was work
related. Is that right?

50
00:03:50,483 --> 00:03:51,235
[HOLMES]
Mm-hm!

51
00:03:51,276 --> 00:03:53,904
Can't imagine who told you that.

52
00:03:53,946 --> 00:03:55,572
I heard something
about living free

53
00:03:55,614 --> 00:03:57,323
in a house with a proper roof.

54
00:03:57,365 --> 00:03:59,993
You're in a worse financial
position than he is!

55
00:04:00,035 --> 00:04:01,578
Wha...?

56
00:04:01,620 --> 00:04:03,914
[HUDSON growls]
You think I'm daft?

57
00:04:03,956 --> 00:04:06,332
You're selecting unfit
candidates on purpose!

58
00:04:06,374 --> 00:04:08,877
You planned this!
An orchestrated waste of time!

59
00:04:08,919 --> 00:04:10,545
Since you're making
this so difficult,

60
00:04:10,587 --> 00:04:12,965
you go find one or
you're out on the street!

61
00:04:13,006 --> 00:04:15,092
[HOLMES sighs]

62
00:04:15,134 --> 00:04:16,969
I've nowhere else to turn.

63
00:04:17,010 --> 00:04:20,305
Please, Stamford, isn't there
anything else you can do?

64
00:04:20,346 --> 00:04:23,892
Me? Well, let me ruminate.

65
00:04:23,934 --> 00:04:27,646
Hold on. He might
need somewhere to stay.

66
00:04:27,687 --> 00:04:29,022
[HOLMES]
Hm?

67
00:04:35,821 --> 00:04:38,782
[WATSON]
Baker Street, two, two, one, B.

68
00:04:40,784 --> 00:04:42,244
This is it.

69
00:04:53,005 --> 00:04:56,382
That's odd. They should've
been expecting me.

70
00:04:59,011 --> 00:05:00,804
[WATSON]
Beg your pardon. [grunts]

71
00:05:00,846 --> 00:05:03,849
That odor. Where's
it coming from?

72
00:05:04,975 --> 00:05:08,770
Excuse me, but is
anybody up there?

73
00:05:11,982 --> 00:05:13,317
[WATSON]
Hello?

74
00:05:15,319 --> 00:05:16,820
[grunts]

75
00:05:16,862 --> 00:05:18,697
[gasps] Good lord!

76
00:05:18,738 --> 00:05:21,241
Are you all right?

77
00:05:21,283 --> 00:05:25,037
His pulse is normal.
Body temperature's unaffected.

78
00:05:26,579 --> 00:05:28,874
Nothing's blocking his airway.

79
00:05:30,083 --> 00:05:32,544
No sign of an external injury.

80
00:05:32,585 --> 00:05:35,672
Yet, blood is exiting the body.

81
00:05:35,714 --> 00:05:38,133
Tell me, what's the time?

82
00:05:38,175 --> 00:05:39,509
[WATSON gasps]

83
00:05:41,511 --> 00:05:43,847
Blast! It can't be
that late already!

84
00:05:43,889 --> 00:05:46,058
Must've dozed off!

85
00:05:46,099 --> 00:05:48,185
I, um... Pardon,
are you all right?

86
00:05:48,227 --> 00:05:49,102
You're bleeding.

87
00:05:49,144 --> 00:05:51,813
Oh, this? Just a
spot of cow's blood.

88
00:05:53,439 --> 00:05:56,068
[HOLMES] Absolutely smashing!
There was a reaction!

89
00:05:56,109 --> 00:05:57,861
The experiment was a success!

90
00:05:57,903 --> 00:05:59,821
The what?

91
00:05:59,863 --> 00:06:01,907
I've created an agent
that precipitates

92
00:06:01,948 --> 00:06:04,450
when it comes into
contact with blood.

93
00:06:04,492 --> 00:06:07,037
This test allows you
detect its presence,

94
00:06:07,079 --> 00:06:10,123
even the smallest of
stains several months old.

95
00:06:10,165 --> 00:06:13,085
[WATSON]
Hmm. Fascinating.

96
00:06:13,126 --> 00:06:15,254
[HOLMES] Someday it'll be common
practice.

97
00:06:15,295 --> 00:06:17,047
There's no telling the number of
murder cases

98
00:06:17,089 --> 00:06:18,715
that this will end up
overturning.

99
00:06:18,757 --> 00:06:20,175
Despite their similarities,

100
00:06:20,217 --> 00:06:22,468
the tincture of guaiac method
is far less reliable,

101
00:06:22,510 --> 00:06:25,471
requires more work, and takes
longer to give you results.

102
00:06:25,513 --> 00:06:28,267
Whereas this te--

103
00:06:28,308 --> 00:06:29,309
Just who are you?

104
00:06:29,351 --> 00:06:32,437
Oh! Sorry.

105
00:06:32,478 --> 00:06:34,647
My name's John H. Watson.

106
00:06:34,689 --> 00:06:37,192
Am I safe to presume
you're Sherlock Holmes?

107
00:06:37,234 --> 00:06:39,403
My friend Stamford says
that you have a room to let.

108
00:06:39,444 --> 00:06:43,115
[HOLMES] Hmm. Home from
the Afghan War, Doctor?

109
00:06:43,156 --> 00:06:44,991
How did you know?

110
00:06:45,033 --> 00:06:47,869
Using simple deduction,
my dear fellow.

111
00:06:47,911 --> 00:06:50,914
Upon discovering me,
you went to check my pulse,

112
00:06:50,956 --> 00:06:55,127
then my breathing, attempting
to diagnosis my malady.

113
00:06:55,168 --> 00:06:58,630
They were the actions of someone
trained in the medical arts.

114
00:06:58,671 --> 00:07:02,134
Furthermore, you show
signs of a sunburn.

115
00:07:02,175 --> 00:07:04,010
It'd be difficult
achieving that complexion

116
00:07:04,052 --> 00:07:06,430
in London this time of year.

117
00:07:06,471 --> 00:07:09,141
John H. Watson, medic
stationed in Afghanistan,

118
00:07:09,182 --> 00:07:11,726
retired and retuned home.

119
00:07:11,768 --> 00:07:13,186
The pocket watch
I checked earlier

120
00:07:13,228 --> 00:07:16,106
is the one that is issued upon
discharge from the army.

121
00:07:16,148 --> 00:07:17,983
Now you're in need
of a place to live.

122
00:07:18,024 --> 00:07:21,111
But residing in a hotel
on a soldier's pension?

123
00:07:21,153 --> 00:07:23,905
Obviously sharing a
flat is more practical.

124
00:07:23,947 --> 00:07:26,532
Thus, why you are here.

125
00:07:26,574 --> 00:07:29,369
My, good show.
Well deduced, sir.

126
00:07:29,411 --> 00:07:31,371
As you've said,
I am indeed a medic

127
00:07:31,413 --> 00:07:33,539
recently home
from the Afghan War.

128
00:07:33,581 --> 00:07:36,084
And if I might add,
quite happily so.

129
00:07:36,126 --> 00:07:37,461
Good show?

130
00:07:37,502 --> 00:07:39,129
Indeed. Maybe write,

131
00:07:39,171 --> 00:07:42,174
oh, something like, "My new
flatmate is bloody brilliant."

132
00:07:42,215 --> 00:07:43,925
How's that?

133
00:07:43,967 --> 00:07:46,303
You are an avid writer?

134
00:07:46,345 --> 00:07:49,431
The ink smudges on your
right thumbnail tell the story.

135
00:07:49,473 --> 00:07:51,683
[WATSON]
Yes. I am.

136
00:07:51,724 --> 00:07:53,977
[HOLMES]
Well, we'd best be off.

137
00:07:54,019 --> 00:07:56,146
Off? Where to?

138
00:07:56,188 --> 00:07:58,023
See our landlady, Miss Hudson.

139
00:07:58,064 --> 00:07:59,524
She's invited us
to dinner to conduct

140
00:07:59,565 --> 00:08:01,193
a formal interview with you.

141
00:08:01,234 --> 00:08:05,030
By all means, lead the way.
Also, please call me John.

142
00:08:05,071 --> 00:08:08,700
All right, John.
I don't see any issue with you,

143
00:08:08,741 --> 00:08:11,077
but ultimately, it's up
to dear Miss Hudson

144
00:08:11,119 --> 00:08:13,372
to decide the final verdict.

145
00:08:15,748 --> 00:08:17,459
A Toast to John!

146
00:08:17,501 --> 00:08:19,627
And also, finally being
able to split the rent!

147
00:08:19,669 --> 00:08:22,714
That's contingent upon
on whether he gets my okay.

148
00:08:22,755 --> 00:08:24,132
Of course.

149
00:08:24,174 --> 00:08:27,135
And we're here to discuss the
matter sensibly over alcohol.

150
00:08:27,177 --> 00:08:29,095
[GROUP]
Cheers!

151
00:08:36,811 --> 00:08:38,980
My, that's quite
a thirst you have.

152
00:08:39,022 --> 00:08:39,898
Had a rough day?

153
00:08:39,939 --> 00:08:41,233
[HOLMES]
Well, you see,

154
00:08:41,274 --> 00:08:43,818
Miss Hudson's under considerable
stress for some reason.

155
00:08:43,860 --> 00:08:45,278
And are you able to deduce

156
00:08:45,320 --> 00:08:48,614
where the majority of
that stress comes from?!

157
00:08:48,656 --> 00:08:51,951
I-I have some inkling
as to its source.

158
00:08:51,993 --> 00:08:53,328
But, ma'am, still,

159
00:08:53,370 --> 00:08:55,955
Sherlock does possess
an incredible talent.

160
00:08:55,997 --> 00:08:57,165
Frankly,

161
00:08:57,207 --> 00:08:59,667
his powers of observation
rendered me speechless.

162
00:08:59,709 --> 00:09:01,920
He has a gift for perspicacity.

163
00:09:01,961 --> 00:09:03,671
I know. Though, it's shame

164
00:09:03,713 --> 00:09:05,798
he chooses to squander
his abilities.

165
00:09:05,840 --> 00:09:07,384
When I think about how the world

166
00:09:07,426 --> 00:09:09,135
won't be improved by
his cunning mind,

167
00:09:09,177 --> 00:09:10,429
it makes me positively--

168
00:09:10,470 --> 00:09:12,138
Just where're you going?!

169
00:09:12,180 --> 00:09:13,973
Just off to the loo, mum.

170
00:09:14,015 --> 00:09:15,767
Oh? Trying to run away.

171
00:09:15,808 --> 00:09:17,269
I swear I'll return.

172
00:09:17,310 --> 00:09:18,270
Although, if you'd prefer,

173
00:09:18,311 --> 00:09:20,605
we could make it
a more communal affair.

174
00:09:20,646 --> 00:09:21,565
[HUDSON gasps]

175
00:09:21,606 --> 00:09:23,233
Holmes! Please!

176
00:09:23,275 --> 00:09:27,195
[HUDSON] Honestly! The man
has nary a shred of decency!

177
00:09:27,237 --> 00:09:28,530
If I might ask,

178
00:09:28,572 --> 00:09:31,157
Miss Hudson, just what
sort of man is Sherlock?

179
00:09:31,199 --> 00:09:32,492
I couldn't tell you!

180
00:09:32,534 --> 00:09:35,454
On my life, I will never
understand what makes him tick.

181
00:09:35,495 --> 00:09:37,122
His mind's constantly
drifting off,

182
00:09:37,163 --> 00:09:38,457
thinking about who knows what.

183
00:09:38,498 --> 00:09:40,459
Often, he won't come home
for two or three days,

184
00:09:40,500 --> 00:09:41,709
no explanation!

185
00:09:41,751 --> 00:09:43,795
Other times, he'll lock
himself away in his office,

186
00:09:43,836 --> 00:09:46,423
ignoring me. The cruelty.

187
00:09:46,465 --> 00:09:51,344
It is true, he can be kind
on the rarest of occasions.

188
00:09:51,386 --> 00:09:54,514
[HUDSON grunts] But that
doesn't give him a pass

189
00:09:54,556 --> 00:09:55,765
for acting like a child!

190
00:09:55,807 --> 00:09:57,601
Which is why I must
insist his housemate

191
00:09:57,642 --> 00:10:00,270
be a fully grounded individual!

192
00:10:00,312 --> 00:10:04,149
[WATSON] You worry about him,
don't you, Miss Hudson?

193
00:10:04,190 --> 00:10:06,943
Almost as if you were family.

194
00:10:06,985 --> 00:10:10,405
[HUDSON grunts, chuckles]

195
00:10:10,447 --> 00:10:12,782
Well, time to survey the damage.

196
00:10:12,824 --> 00:10:14,784
Hope John's okay.

197
00:10:14,826 --> 00:10:17,245
[HUDSON] You mean that
actually happened to you?

198
00:10:17,287 --> 00:10:20,373
My, you really did have a
time of it on the battlefield.

199
00:10:20,415 --> 00:10:21,749
Oh, no.

200
00:10:21,791 --> 00:10:25,587
Nothing compared to the soldiers
who took up their rifles.

201
00:10:25,629 --> 00:10:27,255
Get lost on your way back?

202
00:10:27,297 --> 00:10:29,257
Um, yeah.

203
00:10:29,299 --> 00:10:33,219
So you two seem like you're
getting along swimmingly.

204
00:10:33,261 --> 00:10:35,972
[HUDSON] Sure. Mister Watson's
a down-to-earth gentleman,

205
00:10:36,014 --> 00:10:37,474
unlike you.

206
00:10:41,978 --> 00:10:45,190
[LESTRADE] Forgive me for
interrupting your festivities.

207
00:10:45,231 --> 00:10:47,025
Lestrade!

208
00:10:47,066 --> 00:10:50,069
Beg your pardon,
but who is this?

209
00:10:50,111 --> 00:10:52,947
[HUDSON] Mister Watson,
allow me to introduce

210
00:10:52,989 --> 00:10:55,783
Inspector Lestrade
of Scotland Yard.

211
00:10:55,825 --> 00:10:57,369
The police?

212
00:10:57,410 --> 00:11:00,163
[HOLMES squeals]

213
00:11:00,205 --> 00:11:04,292
Yes! Payday has arrived!

214
00:11:04,334 --> 00:11:06,044
Afraid you lost me.

215
00:11:06,085 --> 00:11:08,713
[HOLMES] You see this
is my half of the rent.

216
00:11:08,754 --> 00:11:10,882
Whenever the police have
a case beyond their wits,

217
00:11:10,923 --> 00:11:12,717
they eventually reach out to me.

218
00:11:12,758 --> 00:11:14,760
I then step in and solve it

219
00:11:14,802 --> 00:11:17,889
as the world's foremost
consulting detective.

220
00:11:19,140 --> 00:11:21,100
That's your profession?

221
00:11:21,142 --> 00:11:24,729
Well, let's be off. No point
standing around here.

222
00:11:24,770 --> 00:11:26,606
So where's the crime scene?

223
00:11:26,648 --> 00:11:28,816
Walking distance
or carriage ride?

224
00:11:28,858 --> 00:11:32,362
Sorry, Holmes, but this
case is slightly different.

225
00:11:32,404 --> 00:11:34,280
Huh?

226
00:11:34,322 --> 00:11:37,534
Tonight, at about
roughly ten PM,

227
00:11:37,576 --> 00:11:40,579
the murdered body of
Lord Drebber was discovered.

228
00:11:40,620 --> 00:11:43,623
Before the man died,
he left us a clue--

229
00:11:43,665 --> 00:11:46,918
a message next to his body,
written in blood.

230
00:11:46,959 --> 00:11:50,505
Just one word, "Sherlock."

231
00:11:51,464 --> 00:11:54,967
[HOLMES]
Really?

232
00:11:55,009 --> 00:12:00,390
I want you to know it gives me
no pleasure doing this.

233
00:12:00,432 --> 00:12:02,308
But, Sherlock Holmes,

234
00:12:02,350 --> 00:12:05,604
I'm placing you under arrest
on the suspicion of murder.

235
00:12:05,645 --> 00:12:07,439
Don't make a scene.

236
00:12:14,070 --> 00:12:16,322
[HOLMES laughing]

237
00:12:16,364 --> 00:12:18,366
The utter genius!

238
00:12:18,408 --> 00:12:21,703
The victim writing my name
in blood! That's rich!

239
00:12:21,745 --> 00:12:22,870
Good show.

240
00:12:22,912 --> 00:12:25,290
We were together at
the estimated time of death.

241
00:12:25,331 --> 00:12:27,459
Meaning he has a solid alibi.

242
00:12:27,500 --> 00:12:29,628
That's why we're
taking you all in.

243
00:12:29,669 --> 00:12:32,880
Honestly, Scotland Yard's
always easily duped.

244
00:12:32,922 --> 00:12:36,593
You find my name at the crime
scene and think I'm the culprit.

245
00:12:38,678 --> 00:12:40,388
We'll see how long
your arrogance holds

246
00:12:40,430 --> 00:12:42,181
given the mountain of evidence.

247
00:12:42,223 --> 00:12:45,101
Ah. That explains it.

248
00:12:45,143 --> 00:12:47,437
Who else but you, Gregson?

249
00:12:47,479 --> 00:12:48,563
Nothing thrills me more

250
00:12:48,605 --> 00:12:51,149
than to finally see your
smug face behind bars.

251
00:12:51,190 --> 00:12:52,275
Damn bohemian.

252
00:12:52,317 --> 00:12:53,610
You do realize the reason

253
00:12:53,652 --> 00:12:55,236
the Yard's so
incompetent is 'cause

254
00:12:55,278 --> 00:12:58,323
it's overrun with insufferable
prats like yourself?

255
00:12:58,364 --> 00:13:00,617
You'll be an assistant
inspector forever

256
00:13:00,659 --> 00:13:03,787
if that mystery eludes you.

257
00:13:03,828 --> 00:13:05,997
[GREGSON] Show Mister Holmes
to his new quarters.

258
00:13:06,038 --> 00:13:08,082
[OFFICERS]
Sir.

259
00:13:08,124 --> 00:13:10,335
[GREGSON]
As for your two witnesses,

260
00:13:10,376 --> 00:13:13,463
there's a few matters
we'd like to ask them about.

261
00:13:17,509 --> 00:13:19,260
Take a good look.

262
00:13:19,302 --> 00:13:21,971
All these were recovered
from his residence.

263
00:13:22,013 --> 00:13:24,432
They belong to our suspect.

264
00:13:24,474 --> 00:13:26,225
Not only are the footprints
left at the scene

265
00:13:26,267 --> 00:13:28,353
the same size as Holmes's boots,

266
00:13:28,394 --> 00:13:31,022
but we also found a gun
that was the same calibre

267
00:13:31,063 --> 00:13:33,566
as the one used in the crime.

268
00:13:33,608 --> 00:13:36,653
You know as well as I do
the suspect is a dangerous man

269
00:13:36,695 --> 00:13:39,447
who caused us trouble
on numerous occasions.

270
00:13:39,489 --> 00:13:40,615
It was just a matter of time

271
00:13:40,657 --> 00:13:42,950
before he did
something like this.

272
00:13:45,119 --> 00:13:48,080
[LESTRADE] And you swear,
you weren't responsible?

273
00:13:48,998 --> 00:13:51,501
You know footprints
can be faked, Lestrade.

274
00:13:51,543 --> 00:13:52,544
Not to mention the fact

275
00:13:52,585 --> 00:13:54,962
my pistol's new and
hasn't been fired yet.

276
00:13:55,004 --> 00:13:58,132
Should be easy
enough to confirm.

277
00:13:58,174 --> 00:14:01,511
Obviously, this is
no ordinary case.

278
00:14:03,680 --> 00:14:06,558
[HOLMES] Something else is
at work behind the scenes.

279
00:14:06,599 --> 00:14:08,976
A huge unseen presence.

280
00:14:09,018 --> 00:14:11,062
And they're still out there.

281
00:14:13,857 --> 00:14:17,527
How about it? Let me see
the crime scene, Lestrade.

282
00:14:17,569 --> 00:14:19,445
Just tell 'em you brought
the suspect along

283
00:14:19,487 --> 00:14:21,823
to try and coax
a confession out of him.

284
00:14:21,865 --> 00:14:24,200
No way. You're mad.

285
00:14:24,242 --> 00:14:26,411
You really think
you can solve this?

286
00:14:28,580 --> 00:14:30,415
I've no other choice.

287
00:14:30,456 --> 00:14:33,626
The victim did request me
by name, after all.

288
00:14:39,424 --> 00:14:41,676
[BOTH gasp]

289
00:14:41,718 --> 00:14:43,302
[WATSON]
Sherlock!

290
00:14:43,344 --> 00:14:44,596
Are you all right?

291
00:14:44,637 --> 00:14:47,724
The assistant inspector let
Miss Hudson and myself go.

292
00:14:47,766 --> 00:14:49,183
[HOLMES]
I'm glad to hear it.

293
00:14:49,225 --> 00:14:52,520
I trust Gregson was bull-headed
in his interrogation.

294
00:14:53,646 --> 00:14:55,940
[HOLMES]
John, I can't do this alone.

295
00:14:55,981 --> 00:14:58,234
I'm going to need
your expertise.

296
00:14:58,276 --> 00:14:59,444
[WATSON grunts]

297
00:14:59,485 --> 00:15:00,862
Yes. Please.

298
00:15:00,904 --> 00:15:03,322
I would like to ask you
for the same favor.

299
00:15:03,364 --> 00:15:04,824
Ahem!

300
00:15:06,200 --> 00:15:08,661
[WATSON] If I can be
of any assistance,

301
00:15:08,703 --> 00:15:11,038
rest assured, I will!

302
00:15:11,080 --> 00:15:14,208
Again, I appreciate
your help. Thank you.

303
00:15:22,425 --> 00:15:24,051
[HOLMES]
Seems the street is lined

304
00:15:24,093 --> 00:15:25,929
with aristocratic mansions.

305
00:15:25,970 --> 00:15:28,807
[GREGSON] What powers
of deduction. Move along.

306
00:15:30,308 --> 00:15:33,436
[LESTRADE] Welcome back to
Lord Drebber's study, Holmes.

307
00:15:34,813 --> 00:15:36,648
[LESTRADE] The crime
scene's been preserved.

308
00:15:36,689 --> 00:15:38,983
We haven't taken
the body to the morgue.

309
00:15:53,915 --> 00:15:57,460
Right, Lestrade.
Tell me everything you know.

310
00:15:58,628 --> 00:16:02,131
Victim's name:
A one Enoch J Drebber.

311
00:16:02,173 --> 00:16:04,968
Born in Wales, 35 years old.

312
00:16:05,009 --> 00:16:08,262
There are two maids and a butler
who live here in the mansion.

313
00:16:08,304 --> 00:16:10,097
Oh, right. I forgot.

314
00:16:10,139 --> 00:16:12,517
In a detached room
there's an older woman

315
00:16:12,558 --> 00:16:14,477
who does the cooking.

316
00:16:14,519 --> 00:16:16,270
Of the two live-in maids,

317
00:16:16,312 --> 00:16:18,773
one of them planned to
rendezvous with her lover.

318
00:16:18,815 --> 00:16:22,109
She was outside during
the estimated time of death.

319
00:16:23,862 --> 00:16:26,113
[LESTRADE]
But despite waiting all night,

320
00:16:26,155 --> 00:16:28,407
he never shows up.

321
00:16:28,449 --> 00:16:33,120
Moreover, she's the one
who discovers the body.

322
00:16:33,162 --> 00:16:34,664
As for the other maid,

323
00:16:34,706 --> 00:16:38,250
she received word her father
had suddenly collapsed.

324
00:16:38,292 --> 00:16:41,004
But when she rushed
to her parents' house,

325
00:16:41,045 --> 00:16:45,299
the man exhibited no
physical ailments.

326
00:16:45,341 --> 00:16:46,425
Then there's the butler

327
00:16:46,467 --> 00:16:48,845
who's fond of sipping
a drink between duties.

328
00:16:48,887 --> 00:16:51,055
He typically spends his
nights in his quarters

329
00:16:51,096 --> 00:16:53,057
with a glass of whiskey.

330
00:16:53,098 --> 00:16:56,686
Apparently, this time it was
laced with some sort of drug.

331
00:16:56,728 --> 00:16:59,647
He was sound asleep when
the murder occurred.

332
00:17:01,190 --> 00:17:04,318
[LESTRADE] The cook is an
older woman hard of hearing,

333
00:17:04,360 --> 00:17:07,071
and once she is settled
in her room for the night,

334
00:17:07,112 --> 00:17:10,575
she wouldn't notice if lightning
struck outside the window.

335
00:17:12,827 --> 00:17:16,039
[LESTRADE]
Which brings us to 10:15 PM,

336
00:17:16,080 --> 00:17:19,166
when the maid discovers
the lord slumped over.

337
00:17:19,208 --> 00:17:22,670
In short, you're saying no one
in or around the mansion

338
00:17:22,712 --> 00:17:25,757
heard the sounds of a
gun firing, is that right?

339
00:17:26,966 --> 00:17:30,094
John, would you mind
inspecting the victim's body?

340
00:17:30,135 --> 00:17:31,554
Certainly.

341
00:17:37,894 --> 00:17:40,730
[WATSON] There are four
gunshot wounds to the chest.

342
00:17:40,772 --> 00:17:43,357
And it doesn't appear to be
any signs of a struggle.

343
00:17:43,399 --> 00:17:44,650
Judging by the cavities,

344
00:17:44,692 --> 00:17:46,277
they were likely
caused by a pistol

345
00:17:46,318 --> 00:17:48,195
fired at point-blank range.

346
00:17:48,237 --> 00:17:50,823
Seems like the killer
was aiming for the heart.

347
00:17:50,865 --> 00:17:53,034
Going off the amount
of blood present,

348
00:17:53,076 --> 00:17:54,577
the injuries sustained
by the victim

349
00:17:54,619 --> 00:17:57,496
were harmful enough
to cause him to bleed out.

350
00:17:58,581 --> 00:18:01,542
[WATSON] He would've
died almost immediately.

351
00:18:01,584 --> 00:18:05,212
Without further tests,
that's all I can ascertain. Uh.

352
00:18:05,254 --> 00:18:07,090
Where'd you go?

353
00:18:07,131 --> 00:18:08,925
[HOLMES]
Excellent work, John.

354
00:18:08,967 --> 00:18:10,384
[WATSON]
Is something wrong?

355
00:18:10,426 --> 00:18:12,637
[HOLMES] No. I thought there'd
be some loose change under here.

356
00:18:12,678 --> 00:18:15,807
[GREGSON]
All right! On your feet, clown!

357
00:18:15,848 --> 00:18:18,935
Give him a little leeway,
and this is how he behaves!

358
00:18:18,977 --> 00:18:20,311
Hey, Lestrade,

359
00:18:20,352 --> 00:18:22,981
would you care to hear what
conclusion I was able to draw?

360
00:18:23,022 --> 00:18:25,608
Fine. I figure
even if we say no,

361
00:18:25,650 --> 00:18:28,861
you'll regale us with
your genius anyway.

362
00:18:28,903 --> 00:18:31,405
[HOLMES] First, there's
the writing in blood.

363
00:18:31,447 --> 00:18:35,076
The earl was not the one who
wrote this. It was the culprit.

364
00:18:35,118 --> 00:18:36,494
Your evidence?

365
00:18:36,535 --> 00:18:39,080
It's obvious if you
look a little closer.

366
00:18:39,122 --> 00:18:40,915
The width of the
lettering is clearly wider

367
00:18:40,957 --> 00:18:43,751
than Drebber's bloody finger.

368
00:18:43,793 --> 00:18:45,878
If you assume the
lord was the author,

369
00:18:45,920 --> 00:18:48,923
he would've needed to
trace each line twice.

370
00:18:48,965 --> 00:18:53,052
Hardly the behavior of dying man
with precious few seconds.

371
00:18:53,094 --> 00:18:55,262
Meaning, the murderer
would have had to

372
00:18:55,304 --> 00:18:57,640
have wider fingers
than the victim.

373
00:18:57,682 --> 00:19:00,518
[HOLMES] It would be
the most logical answer.

374
00:19:00,559 --> 00:19:04,063
Finger width is generally
proportional to hand size.

375
00:19:04,105 --> 00:19:06,149
And hand size is
generally proportional

376
00:19:06,190 --> 00:19:07,441
to one's physical size.

377
00:19:07,483 --> 00:19:09,694
So there's a high
degree of probability

378
00:19:09,735 --> 00:19:13,405
the real killer was much
larger in stature than Drebber.

379
00:19:13,447 --> 00:19:15,158
[LESTRADE]
I'm listening.

380
00:19:15,199 --> 00:19:17,535
Next is the manner of death.

381
00:19:17,576 --> 00:19:20,496
A skilled murderer would've
put a bullet in the head.

382
00:19:20,538 --> 00:19:22,665
Then perhaps after
taking careful aim,

383
00:19:22,707 --> 00:19:26,711
and not missing, would've
shot the heart to be sure.

384
00:19:26,752 --> 00:19:28,046
And yet,

385
00:19:28,087 --> 00:19:31,174
as you can see, it's a miracle
they hit any vital organs.

386
00:19:31,215 --> 00:19:33,009
[WATSON]
I had the same thought.

387
00:19:33,051 --> 00:19:36,554
It appears that the shooter was
unskilled in handling firearms.

388
00:19:36,595 --> 00:19:38,681
[HOLMES]
Being unskilled is one thing,

389
00:19:38,723 --> 00:19:40,599
but the scattershot
approach indicates

390
00:19:40,641 --> 00:19:43,519
they were firing
blindly at their target.

391
00:19:43,561 --> 00:19:46,189
They ensured each of
the servants wasn't present,

392
00:19:46,230 --> 00:19:48,858
arranging the perfect
conditions for the crime,

393
00:19:48,900 --> 00:19:52,570
only for the actual act
to turn out muddled.

394
00:19:52,611 --> 00:19:55,573
Almost as if this was
an amateur performer

395
00:19:55,614 --> 00:19:57,075
playing the role of a killer

396
00:19:57,116 --> 00:20:00,369
in someone else's
well-crafted script.

397
00:20:01,662 --> 00:20:04,623
Basically, even though
only one individual

398
00:20:04,665 --> 00:20:07,543
was responsible for pulling
the trigger on the gun,

399
00:20:07,585 --> 00:20:10,213
another more capable
person set the stage,

400
00:20:10,254 --> 00:20:11,630
making the crime possible.

401
00:20:11,672 --> 00:20:12,924
- -[GREGSON, WATSON gasp]
- -[LESTRADE] Hold on!

402
00:20:12,965 --> 00:20:14,675
In other words, a mastermind.

403
00:20:14,717 --> 00:20:17,553
And someone besides the
killer came up with the plan.

404
00:20:17,595 --> 00:20:20,932
[HOLMES]
Indeed. A puppet master.

405
00:20:20,973 --> 00:20:23,809
The one pulling all the strings.

406
00:20:23,851 --> 00:20:25,728
Perhaps...

407
00:20:25,770 --> 00:20:29,148
...the same as the
case on the Noahtic.

408
00:20:29,190 --> 00:20:35,196
A colossal mosaic
I'm not yet able to see.

409
00:20:35,238 --> 00:20:36,781
[LESTRADE]
Holmes.

410
00:20:36,822 --> 00:20:37,823
Holmes!

411
00:20:37,865 --> 00:20:39,366
[HOLMES gasps]

412
00:20:39,408 --> 00:20:41,244
Now then, according
to your theory,

413
00:20:41,285 --> 00:20:43,913
someone instructed the real
murderer to write your name,

414
00:20:43,955 --> 00:20:45,915
but why you of all people?

415
00:20:45,957 --> 00:20:48,793
Ah, yes. That answer
will become evident

416
00:20:48,834 --> 00:20:51,003
as soon we apprehend
them and ask.

417
00:20:51,045 --> 00:20:54,257
As soon as--?! I'm sick
of all your double-talk!

418
00:20:54,298 --> 00:20:55,299
Inspector!

419
00:20:55,340 --> 00:20:58,010
I request permission
to interrogate Holmes!

420
00:20:58,052 --> 00:20:59,845
We have our man.

421
00:20:59,887 --> 00:21:01,180
I'll do whatever it takes to
make sure he confesses!

422
00:21:01,222 --> 00:21:02,514
Here.

423
00:21:16,279 --> 00:21:19,073
[WILLIAM] I can help
you exact your revenge.

424
00:21:19,115 --> 00:21:23,161
But to do it, you'll have
to sacrifice your life.

425
00:21:23,202 --> 00:21:24,578
Knowing this,

426
00:21:24,620 --> 00:21:27,331
do you remain steadfast
in your resolve?

427
00:21:27,372 --> 00:21:29,083
[HOPE]
Sooner or later,

428
00:21:29,125 --> 00:21:32,377
I'm gonna fall victim to
this incurable disease.

429
00:21:32,419 --> 00:21:36,340
My time is limited,
but none of that's important,

430
00:21:36,381 --> 00:21:38,801
as long as my wish comes true.

431
00:21:40,011 --> 00:21:43,222
[HOPE]
My life means nothing.

432
00:21:45,641 --> 00:21:50,271
[WILLIAM] He walks alone,
death his only companion--

433
00:21:50,313 --> 00:21:53,858
the best candidate
for executing this plan.

434
00:21:53,899 --> 00:21:57,569
And my chance
to finally test him.

435
00:23:34,917 --> 00:23:37,502
[WATSON] Next time
on Moriarty the Patriot  .

436
00:23:37,544 --> 00:23:40,298
"A Study in 'S' 'Act Two.'"
